    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d914">

                <pb facs="1076" n="981" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg981"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.797/17: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-01-31T11:00:00-05:00">January 31, 1936—11
                        a.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>14. Your 6, January 9, 11 a.m., second paragraph, and your despatch 1631, January
                        9.<anchor n="44" xml:id="d914-f1"/> For your information the Department <pb
                        facs="1077" n="982" xml:id="pg982"/> considers that the use of the word
                    “manufactories” in its context in Articles I and II of the Treaty confers on
                    American citizens the indisputable right to manufacture on terms of complete
                    equality with Japanese subjects.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
